(project tuna-sharing
 (* agent note (|2026-09-17, F1 follow-through: the crown growth
    experiment measured no-sharing reduction cost at exactly
    2^(d+1)-2 steps for the T-family (T0=I, Ti = lam z. T(i-1) (T(i-1) z))
    while the result stayed 5 nodes - the exponential is in the
    FIRING count, not the answer.  Roerick's F1 call is the engine
    split; this chapter is the optimized side: a second run
    semantics, recorded as a run-row version, verifiable under its
    own law.  The canonical engine stays the normative counter: the
    differential corpus and three-engine step-count agreement remain
    canonical-only, and v1 step counts are not comparable to v0 and
    not claimed to be.

   Update 2026-09-18: the owner took the call - engine split
   accepted, F1 recorded OBSERVED in FINDINGS.md; v0 stays the API
   default and the memo stays per-run for now; cross-run
   demand-memo is planned below (owner-interested, not scheduled).|) *)
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: sharing - the distinct-work semantics (v1): memoized firings, finite loop answers, prim exemption, and per-version replay. One evaluator, two accounting laws.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section counting-law
  (doc "What a step is under v1")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        v0 counts every firing of a triage rule (AGENTS.md rule 4).  v1
        counts every DISTINCT firing: a firing is the pair (fun-tree,
        arg-tree) identified by content hash (sha256 over the canonical
        ternary form, the same address law as tree_values).  The first
        firing of a pair costs one step and one fuel and records its
        answer in a per-run memo table; every later firing of the SAME
        pair is a memo hit - it returns the recorded answer and costs
        nothing.  Fuel is therefore the distinct-work budget: a v1 run
        with fuel N performs at most N distinct firings, however many
        raw firings the term contains.

        Everything else is v0 verbatim: same triage rules, same
        leftmost-innermost order, wrapper applications free, prim calls
        free at the gate, same size_cap checks at the same points.
        The memo table is per-run state - never shared across runs,
        because prim answers are per-run facts (grant state, kv rows,
        the allowlist) and a cross-run table would cache a lie.

        Consequence (the honest one): a v1 step count is not a v0 step
        count and neither is reducible to the other.  The T-family
        closed term compiles+evaluates in 2^(d+1)-2 raw firings but
        O(d) distinct ones; omega never finishes under v0 and is a
        finite loop answer under v1 (loop-law below).  This is the F1
        split recorded as a semantics VERSION, not hidden as an
        optimization: the run row says which law produced its numbers.
       |))))
 (section loop-law
  (doc "In-flight re-entry is divergence, answered finitely")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Under leftmost-innermost firing, re-entering a firing (f, x)
        that is still in flight means the computation recurses on its
        own subproblem with nothing in between - genuine divergence,
        and v0 can only report it as fuel exhaustion after burning the
        whole budget.  v1 detects it at the first re-entry: the
        in-flight set holds every key whose firing has started but not
        completed; hitting one aborts evaluation with a new result
        variant Loop (steps taken so far) and run status "loop".

        The answer is finite and deterministic: omega terminates after
        a couple of steps with status loop instead of
        fuel_exhausted after N.  Detection costs no fuel.  The abort
        law matches the other exhaustions exactly: a budget-shaped
        result at the boundary, journaled normally, replayed
        deterministically.  No expressive power is lost relative to
        v0 - every term that loops under v1 burns fuel forever under
        v0, so v1's loop set is a subset of v0's exhaustion set.
       |))))
 (section prim-exemption
  (doc "Side-effecting work never memoizes (the dirty rule)")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        A prim call is a host event, not a calculus step: the gate
        application goes to the host every time, exactly as in v0, and
        is never a memo key.  The stronger rule is about COMPOSITE
        firings: if a prim was answered anywhere inside a firing's
        evaluation, that firing's answer is not memoized - it
        re-executes on every occurrence, re-answering its prims.

        The reason is grant liveness, not replay: the run boundary
        re-checks every prim call against live grant state (revoked
        mid-run bites per call) and journals every call as the audit
        trail.  A memoized firing containing a prim would silently
        skip the re-check and the journal row on its second occurrence
        - a weakening of the grant model dressed up as an
        optimization.  Dirty firings pay for their effects every time;
        clean firings (pure calculus, the common case and the whole
        point of the crown family) memoize freely.

        Replay identity survives on either reading - the journal-fed
        replay consumes prim answers in the same order as the original
        run precisely BECAUSE dirty firings never memoize - but the
        dirty rule stands on the grant law alone.
       |))))
 (section semantics-version
  (doc "The run row says which law produced its numbers")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        runs gains a semantics column (migration 0009): text, NOT
        NULL, DEFAULT 'v0'.  'v0' is the canonical law (raw firing
        count, the only law that ever produced a row before this
        migration).  'v1' is the distinct-work law above.  Every
        existing row reads as v0 without backfill.

        A run request carries an optional semantics field; absent
        means v0, an unknown value is a 400.  The REPL surface takes
        the same field.  The engine takes the mode from the row, not
        from ambient configuration: a row is self-describing, and no
        server restart can change what its numbers mean.
       |))))
 (section replay-per-version
  (doc "Verify replays the row under its own law")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Faithful replay reads the run row's semantics and re-executes
        under that law with the journal-fed host; the four identity
        checks are unchanged in shape (result hash, step count,
        per-seq journal answers consumed in order, hash chain).  A v0
        row verifies against the canonical engine; a v1 row verifies
        against the memoized engine; both are identity proofs, each
        within its own law.

        Not claimed, on purpose: cross-engine step-count equality.
        That was v0's invariant under a single engine and it stays
        true where it was true - the differential corpus and the
        three-engine harness remain canonical-only.  F1's predicted
        consequence ("optimized-unverifiable") is weaker than what
        shipped: the split exists, and verification followed the row
        instead of dying.
       |))))
 (section costs-and-limits
  (doc "What v1 costs and what it does not cover")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Hashing: memo keys are content hashes (sha256 over canonical
        form).  Computing one per firing from scratch would be
        quadratic in the tree; the engine keeps a side table keyed on
        physical node identity (persistent trees share subterm objects
        exactly when the sharing we are harvesting is real) and pays
        each distinct node object one canonical encode + digest.
        Physically-distinct-but-equal subtrees just hash twice;
        correctness never depends on the physical fast path.

        Scope: this chapter covers the RUN engine.  Compile-time
        reduction stays canonical (raw counting under the wall-clock
        cap) - the crown experiment also showed the COMPILE artifact
        doubling per def level, so full crown economics need sharing
        to reach the artifact/tree representation too; that is future
        work, recorded here so the absence is legible.  The memo
        table costs O(distinct answers) memory per run.
         |))))
 (section demand-memo
  (doc "Cross-run sharing of clean firings (planned; owner-interested 2026-09-18, not scheduled)")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        v1's memo is per-run on purpose: prim answers are per-run facts
        (grant state, kv rows, the allowlist), so the whole table
        stays private.  But clean firings are pure calculus - their
        answers are functions of the content digest alone, which
        makes them SHAREABLE across runs and, later, across
        instances.  A cross-run demand-memo keys the same content
        digests into a shared store: the first demander pays the full
        firing; later demanders of the same pair reuse the recorded
        answer.  The dirty rule hardens into a boundary law: only
        firings whose evaluation never touched a prim may enter the
        shared table, so grant liveness and the audit trail are
        untouched by construction - the shared table is pure-cache
        only.

        Open with the owner: the trust model (whose cached answers
        may a run accept - own-garden only, verified-only where a
        cached answer must carry a replay receipt, or anyone's), the
        price of a cache hit (free like v1's per-run hits, or a
        metered read), and the replay story (a row that consumed
        shared answers replays under which law when the shared table
        has since evolved - current leaning: replay re-executes and
        treats the shared table as a host, so identity stays
        per-run).  Federation interplay: the shared memo is the
        natural export surface once M12 exists; sequencing
        demand-memo after federation keeps the trust question from
        being answered twice.
       |))))
)
